---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/projects/ProjectHeader.astro';
import FincrimeDiagram from '../../components/projects/diagrams/FincrimeDiagram.astro';
import TraceReplay from '../../components/projects/TraceReplay.astro';
import { getProject } from '../../data/projects';

const project = getProject('fincrime-investigator-agent');
---

<Layout
	title={`${project.title} | Emad Gohari`}
	description={project.description}
	image={project.ogImage}
	imageAlt={project.title}
	type="article"
>
	<article class="w-full py-16 fade-in-up">
		<ProjectHeader project={project} />

		<div class="prose">
			<h2>The problem</h2>
			<p>
				Given an entity (a name, optionally a type, birth date and countries), a compliance analyst needs a risk
				memo they can trust: a risk level and why, sanctions exposure, ownership and control exposure including
				indirect ownership, key relationships, what the company's filings say, and open questions.
			</p>
			<p>
				Getting an LLM to write a plausible memo is easy. The hard part is making it <strong>reliably correct and
				auditable</strong>: no invented relationships, no under-called risk, no obeying instructions hidden inside a
				filing, every factual sentence traceable to evidence that was actually retrieved, and behaviour that is
				measured on a fixed test set rather than eyeballed.
			</p>

			<h2>What I built</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					An explicit <strong>LangGraph state machine</strong>: deterministic nodes screen the entity, walk the
					ownership graph and search SEC filings; a <code>policy</code> node computes a <strong>risk floor</strong>
					from rules; the LLM writes a structured memo that cites evidence ids; a verifier checks it.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong>verifier</strong> with eight deterministic checks (citations exist, every claim is cited, risk is
					at least the floor, confirmed matches equal the adjudicated set, the sanctions status is consistent with them, quarantined text is never used as
					support, no unknown entities, absence claims cite the search that supports them) plus an LLM check that
					each claim is supported by the text it cites. One retry with feedback, then a deterministic repair that
					marks the memo <code>needs_review</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Layered prompt-injection defences</strong>: input sanitising, paragraph-level redaction of
					retrieved filing text, <code>&lt;untrusted_document&gt;</code> prompt structure, and a verifier that
					ignores anything uncited.
				</li>
				<li>
					Light <strong>RAG over SEC filings</strong>: SQLite FTS5 for BM25, embeddings computed lazily for the BM25
					pool only, reciprocal rank fusion. No vector database to operate.
				</li>
				<li>
					An <strong>MCP server</strong> exposing the same typed tools to Claude Code and Claude Desktop, a FastAPI
					SSE backend with a replay UI, OpenTelemetry tracing with token and cost accounting, and a
					<strong>63-case golden set</strong> with an eval runner and an LLM-as-judge calibrated against hand
					labels.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Architecture</h2>
			<FincrimeDiagram />
		</div>

		<section id="demo" class="mt-14 scroll-mt-8">
			<h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-text-primary">Try it: replay a recorded investigation</h2>
			<p class="mb-6 leading-7 text-text-secondary">
				These are real runs of the agent with <code class="rounded border border-border-subtle bg-bg-card px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-accent">gemini-3.8-flash</code>,
				recorded on 11 October 2026 and replayed here step by step. Nothing runs on a server and no model is called
				while you watch. Two of the cases use fictional companies and filings built to test the ownership rule and
				prompt-injection handling; in two runs you can see the verifier reject a first draft and the model fix it.
			</p>
			<TraceReplay />
		</section>

		<div class="prose mt-14">
			<h2>Results</h2>
			<p>
				All numbers are produced by the repo's eval runner on the 63-case golden set. "Scripted" replaces the LLM
				with a deterministic baseline writer, so it measures the pipeline (screening, traversal, the ownership
				rule, retrieval, injection handling, verification), not language quality.
			</p>
			<!-- Source: fincrime-investigator-agent/README.md "Results" and evals/results/20261011T083752Z-scripted.md,
			     evals/results/20261011T083809Z-scripted-fullindex.md, evals/results/20261011T083639Z-gemini-3.8-flash.md -->
			<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Metric</th>
							<th class="num">Fixture index, scripted (CI)</th>
							<th class="num">Full OpenSanctions index, scripted</th>
							<th class="num">Fixture index, gemini-3.8-flash</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>Risk-level accuracy</td><td class="num">98.4% (62/63)</td><td class="num">96.8% (61/63)</td><td class="num">98.4% (62/63)</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Under-calls / over-calls</td><td class="num">0 / 1</td><td class="num">0 / 2</td><td class="num">0 / 1</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Sanctions-hit precision / recall</td><td class="num">96.2% / 100%</td><td class="num">96.2% / 100%</td><td class="num">96.2% / 100%</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Entity resolution / ownership detection</td><td class="num">100% / 100%</td><td class="num">100% / 100%</td><td class="num">100% / 100%</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Citation validity (deterministic)</td><td class="num">100%</td><td class="num">100%</td><td class="num">100%</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Verified on first attempt / repaired</td><td class="num">100% / 0</td><td class="num">100% / 0</td><td class="num">87.3% / 1</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Injection cases flagged / text leaks</td><td class="num">3/3 / 0</td><td class="num">3/3 / 0</td><td class="num">3/3 / 0</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Groundedness, judge gemini-3.6-flash (1-5)</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">4.81</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Second judge gemini-3.8-flash: mean, exact agreement</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">4.73, 86%</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Tool calls / LLM calls per case</td><td class="num">8.3 / 1</td><td class="num">8.3 / 1</td><td class="num">8.3 / 2.2</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Tokens in / out per case</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">n/a</td><td class="num">3,981 / 1,643</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Cost per case (list price)</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$0.0091</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Latency p50 / p95</td><td class="num">29 / 130 ms</td><td class="num">85 / 236 ms</td><td class="num">10.2 / 18.0 s</td></tr>
					</tbody>
					<caption>63 cases per column. Each real-model configuration was run once.</caption>
				</table>
			</div>
			<ul>
				<li>
					The risk floor makes under-calls impossible by construction, so for the LLM run the accuracy number
					mostly says the model never over-called. The interesting LLM metrics are groundedness, verification and
					cost.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The verifier does real work.</strong> In the Gemini run, 8 of 63 first drafts failed
					verification: 5 were caught by the in-loop LLM support check (for example a filing date taken from an
					uncited item) and 3 by the absence-claim check. All but one passed on retry.
				</li>
				<li>
					An earlier 24-case run, before the absence-claim check existed, had judge scores of 2 and 3 on no-match
					cases: the model claimed "no ownership linkages" while citing only the screening summary. After adding
					search-coverage evidence and the check, the lowest score in the full run is 3 (2 of 63 cases).
				</li>
				<li>
					The one sanctions false positive in every run is Sberbank, because two listed entities carry the exact
					name "Sberbank of Russia".
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Key design decisions and trade-offs</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Rules decide the floor, the model writes.</strong> In screening, a missed sanctioned owner is a
					regulatory breach while an over-call costs an analyst a few minutes. A rule that cannot be argued out of a
					decision by a prompt, or by text inside a filing, fits that asymmetry. It also makes the whole pipeline
					testable in CI without a model.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Evidence ledger with short ids</strong> (<code>E1</code>…<code>En</code>) assigned as tools return.
					The model cites ids; the verifier and the eval check them. Every search also records its coverage,
					because a negative finding is only as good as the search behind it ("not performed" means unknown, not
					absent).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Bounded, purposeful traversal</strong>: depth 1 for everything with capped fan-out, deeper only up
					ownership chains, because that is what the aggregated 50%-style ownership rule needs. The result is 8.3
					tool calls per case.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An explicit contract gap.</strong> The screening service's relationship data has no direction.
					The agent normalises edges and refuses to use direction-less edges for the ownership rule, which makes it
					more conservative rather than wrong.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Deterministic planning</strong> instead of an LLM planner: an investigation procedure is fixed,
					and a planner adds cost and a failure mode without adding coverage.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Cost controls</strong>: low reasoning effort for the memo writer (the evidence is already
					adjudicated), a replay mode for the public demo, and rate limits plus a daily cap for live mode.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Limitations</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Eval labels follow a written, illustrative policy. The deterministic eval shows the system implements it
					robustly, not that the policy is right.
				</li>
				<li>
					A few thresholds were adjusted after looking at eval output. With 63 cases there is no clean held-out
					split; the full-index run adds distractors that were never tuned on.
				</li>
				<li>
					Public ownership data rarely has percentages, so most real ownership findings are "presumed control"
					pending the actual stake.
				</li>
				<li>
					Filing cases in the golden set are fictional. Live EDGAR retrieval is smoke-tested but has no labelled
					real-filing cases yet.
				</li>
				<li>Real-model numbers come from one run per configuration, judged by Gemini models only.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Data and attribution</h2>
			<p>
				Sanctions, ownership and relationship data:
				<a href="https://www.opensanctions.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenSanctions</a>
				(<code>sanctions</code> collection, snapshot 2026-10-11), licensed
				<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-NC 4.0</a>.
				This is a non-commercial portfolio project; commercial use of the data requires a licence from
				OpenSanctions. Company filings:
				<a href="https://www.sec.gov/edgar" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC EDGAR</a> (public domain),
				accessed with a declared User-Agent within SEC's fair-access rate limit. Entities and filings marked
				synthetic are fictional test fixtures. The compliance logic is illustrative, not legal advice.
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
